# File Formats, part 2: the files that share the container, the files of the
# user, the data files, the pictures and the text files.
.h2 Templates, macro sets, libraries and backups [appfmt-set]
.ix Template file format
.ix .SET file
.ix Settings file, template
A template is a complete document.  A file that Save Global Settings Only
(Reference Manual, chapter 2) makes has the full header and an empty body: the
record {c:*B1}
followed at once by {c:*E}.  A template that has text is the text that a New
File starts with.  Retrieve Global Settings replaces the header records except
{c:*R}, {c:*B} and MR; Retrieve Body Only replaces the body.  The panel of
New File offers the name DEFAULT.SET; when no such file is found the Editor
starts from a built-in document and asks whether it is to be structured or
unstructured.  No other record is special: a template that is a document is a
document.
.h3 Macro sets: .MAC [appfmt-mac]
.ix Macro file format
.ix .MAC file
.ix Key token in a macro
.ix Keystrokes, stored
A macro set is a structured document, written by Tools, Macros, Save.  The
header holds *R2, the description MD, the author MA and the revision MR, in
that order, and the body is made so:
.code
*B1
S                      the root section
T 00                   its empty text
+
S                      the first macro
T K 11 key flag 1D 00  the headline: K, then the key of the macro
T description 1D 00    the description of the macro
+
S
T keys 1D 00           the keystrokes
-
...                    the next macros
-
*E
.end
The key of a macro is an upper case letter or a digit; in the headline it is
written as a key token (below) with the flag byte 80, which means that the
macro is started with the key {k:Alt} and that letter.  Only the macros that
have Include in Macro Set at Yes are written.  The keystrokes of a macro are a
mixture of two kinds of bytes.  A character that was typed without a modifier
is the byte itself.  Every other key is a token of three bytes: 11, a code, and
a flag.
.table 14 56
Flag | Meaning of the key token
00 | A character key; the code is the upper case letter or the character.
40 | The Ctrl key is held with the key (a letter from A to Z as the code).
80 | The Alt key is held.
C0 | Both.
11 | An editing key; the code is 0 Enter, 1 Insert, 2 Delete, 3 Escape,
 | 5 Backspace and 7 Tab.
12 | A function key; the code is its number, 1 to 10.
14 | A cursor key; the code is 0 Up, 1 Down, 2 Left, 3 Right, 4 Page Up, 5
 | Page Down, 6 Home and 7 End.
.end
The kind of key is in the low five bits of the flag (00, 11, 12 and 14 in the
table), and the modifiers in the bits 40 and 80 above them: a flag of 52 is
the function key with the Ctrl key, that is
{k:Ctrl-F3} when the code is 3 (the token 11 03 52), and the token 11 0A 12 is
the key {k:F12}.
Shift is not kept.  A token that Strata does not know is dropped when the file
is read.  A file of a macro set that does not begin {c:*R2} is refused when it
is retrieved.
.caution A macro set is read by its own reader, which ends a T record at a NUL
  that is followed by CR LF, because a key token may have the byte 00 as an
  operand (the code of the Enter key is 0: 11 00 11).  The reader of
  documents ends a record at the first NUL outside a marker ({x:appfmt-rec}),
so it cuts such a record early.  A macro set that has the Enter key in a
  macro is read by Retrieve of the macro commands but does not make the same
  bytes when it is loaded and saved as a document.  Do not open a macro set
  with the Retrieve of the File menu, and do not save it from there.
.h3 Libraries: .LIB [appfmt-lib]
.ix Library file format
.ix .LIB file
.ix Library entries, stored
A library is also a structured document, written by Tools, Library, Save, with
the header records MD, MA and MR.  Each entry is a section of the root, with
two blocks and a group of subsections:
.code
S
T kind name 1D 00      the headline: a letter and the name of the entry
T description 1D 00    the description
+
...                    the contents of the entry
-
.end
The letter of the headline is B for an entry that is a block, S for a section
and T for text.  The contents of a text entry are one section with one block
that holds the text; those of a block entry are one section with the blocks
of the entry; those of a section entry are the sections and the blocks of the
entry as they were, with their formats.  The entries are kept in alphabetical
order.  A library has
at most 200 entries and a macro set has at most 64 macros.  A file that is not
structured, or whose first record is not a section, is refused with "That file
is not a library".
.h3 Backup files: .BAK [appfmt-bak]
.ix Backup file format
.ix .BAK file
.ix Autosave file
A backup is a copy of the document as it was on the disk before Save wrote
the new version: the bytes are the same, so it is a document and can be
retrieved.  Up to the number of backups that the user chose (0 to 99, three by
default) are kept, as numbered files in the folder {c:.backup} in the directory
of the document: the
name of the document without {c:.doc}, then {c:.bak} and the number with two
digits, such as {c:.backup/report.bak01} (the newest) to
{c:.backup/report.bak99} (versions before this one left them beside the
document); {c:.BAK}
when the extension of the document is in capital letters.  A name that does
not end with {c:.doc} gets {c:.bak01} and so on added to the whole name.  A
save moves every backup up by one number, removes the one above the number
chosen and keeps the old file as number 01; it does nothing when the content
is the same as the file on the disk (Reference Manual, chapter 2,
{x:file-backup}).  Autosave is not a file of its own: it writes the document
under its own name, as {k:Ctrl-S} does, and makes no backup.

.h2 The files of the user in ~/.strata [appfmt-strata]
.ix .strata directory, files
.ix config file format
.ix fonts file format
.ix dictionary file format
.ix STRATA_HOME
The directory is {c:~/.strata}, or the directory that the environment variable
{c:STRATA_HOME} names.  It is made with the mode 0700 and its files with the
mode
0600, and a file or a directory that is not of the user is not used
(Reference Manual, appendix B).
.h3 config [appfmt-strata-config]
.ix config, record layout
.ix key=value, settings file
A text file of lines {c:key=value}, in the character set of the system.  The
lines are written in this order, one for each of the settings:
.table 6 24 40
Line | Key | Value
1 | workdir | The initial working directory.
2 | (the name of the Editor program) | The program to start for editing.
3 | help | The help file.
4 | autosave | yes or no.
5 | {c:autosave_seconds} | A number of seconds, 1 to 86400.
6 | {c:expanded_status} | yes or no.
7 | {c:onscreen_help} | yes or no.
8 | {c:show_marks} | yes or no.
9 | {c:show_dots} | yes or no.
10 | backups | A number, 0 to 99; written only when it is not 1.  The key
 backup (yes or no) of an older file is read as 1 or 0 and not written.
11 | printer | The name of a printer queue; empty when none is named.
12 | {c:spell_language} | The language of the spell check; this line is written
 only when a language is set.
13 | {c:latex_pictures} | keep, when the LaTeX file names its pictures in place;
 this line is written only for keep.
14 | {c:latex_positions} | yes, when the LaTeX file keeps its position comments;
 this line is written only for yes.
.end
The line {c:printer=} is always written and is empty when no printer is named.
A yes-or-no value is written as {c:yes} or {c:no}.  A key that the reader does
not know is ignored and the line is lost at the next save.  Appendix B of the
Reference Manual
({x:appcfg-format}) lists the keys with their rules.
.h3 fonts [appfmt-strata-fonts]
.ix fonts, record layout
.ix Font list file
One line for each font, the fields set apart by bars:
.code
NUMBER|NAME|PACKAGE|OPTIONS|FAMILY|ARCHIVE|MAP
5|Helvetica|helvet|scaled=0.92|sans||
19|Lato|lato|default|sans|lato|lato.map
.end
NUMBER is 1 to 20, FAMILY is one of roman, sans and mono, and the other fields
may be empty.  The characters of the package, the options, the archive and the
map are limited to letters, digits and {c:. _ - + = ,}, so that nothing of
the list reaches a shell or the TeX file as a command; the name may be any text
that has no bar.  A line with another number of fields, with a bad number or
family, or with a field that has a character that is not allowed, is ignored.
The whole list is written at every save.  The list that comes with Strata,
and the meaning of every field, are in appendix B of the Reference Manual
({x:appcfg-fonts}).
.h3 dictionary [appfmt-strata-dict]
.ix dictionary, record layout
.ix Personal word list file
.ix Spell check word list
The personal word list of the spell check, one word to a line, in UTF-8, with
a line feed at the end of each line.  A blank line is ignored.  A word that
has a letter that the character set does not have is read with a question mark
in its place.  When the
list is read, a word in lower case is also taken with an initial capital
letter, and a word that has capitals is taken as it is; Define writes the
word as it was typed to the Editor.  The words are not sorted: new words go at
the end.  The file is written whole at every Define.
.h3 hyphenation [appfmt-strata-hyph]
.ix hyphenation, file
.ix Hyphenation list file
.ix Personal hyphenation list file
The personal hyphenation list, one entry to a line, in UTF-8, with a line feed
at the end of each line.  A blank line is ignored.  An entry is a word with a
hyphen at each place where it may be broken: {c:hy-phen-a-tion}.  A word with
no hyphen is never broken; an equals sign in front of it ({c:=Jacksonville})
says so, and no hyphen may follow it.  The letters are letters of the PC
character set, accented ones too; a digit, an apostrophe, a space or a hyphen
at either end or two hyphens in a row make the line no entry, and so does a
character that the fonts of the print do not have.  Such a line is skipped
when the list is read.  Maintain Dictionaries keeps the entries in order of
their letters, without regard to case, and two entries with the same letters
are one word.  The Printer writes the entries as a TeX hyphenation command in
front of the document: the words of the list are broken at the hyphens only.
.h2 Pictures [appfmt-pic]
.ix Picture formats
.ix PNG, JPEG, PDF and EPS pictures
.ix Picture file, kind by content
The Picture marker (Reference Manual, chapter 5) names a file.  The Printer
accepts four kinds:
PNG, JPEG, PDF and EPS (PostScript).  The kind is found from the first bytes of
the file, not from its extension, and then the structure of the file is
checked, so that a damaged file does not stop TeX without a message.
.table 10 24 36
Kind | First bytes | Checked
PNG | 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A | Every chunk and its CRC up to IEND.
JPEG | FF D8 FF | A frame header, and the end marker FF D9.
PDF | the text %PDF | The text %%EOF in the last 1024 bytes.
EPS | the text %!PS, or C5 D0 D3 C6 | The text %%BoundingBox:
.end
A file that is none of these, or that does not pass the check, is printed in
the long form of its marker (a frame with the name), and the run goes on.  The
A file is looked for as the name is
written (a full path, or a path below the
directory of the document), and then by the last part of the name, with any
case of the letters, in the directory of the document and in the current
directory of the Printer; a backslash in the name is read as a slash.  The
Picture panel offers the extensions {c:.png .jpg .jpeg .pdf .eps} in its list.
.h2 The help file [appfmt-help]
.ix Help file format
.ix Help topics, format
The help file is a plain text file, {c:strata.hlp}, made of topics.  The build
makes it by joining the topic files of the source tree, which have the same
format; a person who writes help for Strata writes a topic file.  Every line is
ASCII text.  A line that begins with a mark and a blank is a command; any other
line is text of the topic that the last {c:=} line began.
.table 10 60
Mark | Meaning
{c:=} | Begins a topic and gives its title.
{c:~} | Another name of the topic.  The programs ask for a topic by the title of
 | the menu, panel or field that the cursor is in, for example {c:Save File} or
 | {c:Menu:Print:Draft} or {c:Save File:Options}; a topic answers to its title
 | and to its names.  A name that has no topic is made shorter, by the part from
 | a blank and a parenthesis and by the last part after a colon, until a topic
 | answers.
{c:?} | A message.  When the message line shows a text that begins with it, {k:F1}
 | shows the topic.  A text that begins with {c:N} and a blank stands for a
 | number: {c:N replacement} fits {c:3 replacements}.
{c:;} | A comment, which is not part of the topic.
{c:-} | An item of a list; the lines after it that begin with two blanks belong to
 | it.
.end
A blank line ends a paragraph.  The lines of a paragraph are joined and broken
again at the width of the window.  A line that begins with a vertical bar and a
blank is shown as it is, and is not joined with the lines around it.  In the
text, a key name between braces is shown as a keycap; a name between two pairs
of square brackets is a link to the topic of that name, and a name, a vertical
bar and words between them is a link that shows the words; a brace that is not
the start of a key is written twice.  The title and the names of the topics
are each used by one topic only (the case is ignored).
.h2 The thesaurus data [appfmt-thes]
.ix Thesaurus file format
.ix MyThes files
.ix STRATA_THESAURUS
.ix .idx and .dat files
The thesaurus reads the files of the MyThes thesaurus, as the system packages
for spell checkers have them (a pair, the index NAME.idx and the data
NAME.dat).  Both are text,
and the first line of each names the character set, normally {c:UTF-8}.
.table 12 58
File | Layout
.idx | Line 1 the character set.  Line 2 the number of entries.  Then one line
 | for each entry: the word, a bar, and the decimal offset of its entry in
 | the data file.  The words are in byte order, in lower case.
.dat | Line 1 the character set.  An entry is a line {c:word|n}, then n lines of
 | senses.  A sense line begins with (noun), (verb), (adj) or (adv),
 | then the words set apart by bars.  A word followed by a space and (similar
 | term), (generic term), (antonym) or (related term) is a word of that
 | relation; a word with no relation is a synonym.
.end
The data is looked for in the directory that {c:STRATA_THESAURUS} names (or the
file, or the name without its extension), and when the variable is not set in
{c:/usr/share/mythes}.  A line that does not have the shape above gives "not
found" and not a failure.  With UTF-8 the words are converted to code page 437,
and a word that has a letter that the set lacks is dropped.
.h2 Text files [appfmt-textout]
.ix Text file, made by Export
.ix Export, file format
.ix Text mode of the Editor, file format
Export (Reference Manual, chapter 2) writes bytes of the code page chosen,
each line ended by CR
LF, with the bullet of a list written as an asterisk.  A screen image has
the width of 80 cells and no more than 79 characters in a line, with a blank
line between blocks; data only has one line for a block, tabs as the byte 09,
and no marker but those that have a text.  The text mode of the Editor
(Reference Manual, chapter 10) writes UTF-8, a line feed at the end of each
line, lines of at
most 78 cells with trailing spaces taken off, and a blank line for each rule of
the screen; a character of the document between B0 and DF is {c:+} or {c:-}.
.h2 LaTeX files [appfmt-latex]
.ix LaTeX file, format
.ix Export a LaTeX File, file format
.ix Pictures folder, file format
Export a LaTeX File writes the
LaTeX file that Print and Preview give to LaTeX (Reference Manual, chapter 2,
{x:file-export-latex}), as text in ASCII with the characters above 7F as UTF-8
for the package inputenc.  The lines end with a line feed.  The file has these
parts, in this order.
.table 6 64
1 | A header of comment lines: the words "LaTeX file exported by Strata.", the
 | name of the document, the day, the command that compiles the file, the
 | packages, the folder of the pictures and the warning that the file is made
 | again.
2 | The preamble and the body of the file of a print, without the banner, the
 | marks of errors and the Error Log.  A comment line that begins with
 | {c:%@P} and gives the item, column and line of the document is written
 | before each line of text only when the setting {c:latex_positions} is
 | yes.
3 | The line that ends the LaTeX document.
4 | A list of the pictures, as comment lines: the name that the file uses for
 | the picture, an equals sign and the name that the document has for it.
.end
A picture is written as {c:FOLDER/picN.EXT}, where FOLDER is the name of the
file without {c:.tex} followed by {c:_files} (with the characters other than
letters, digits and {c:._-} replaced by an underscore), N counts the pieces of
the document that LaTeX can refuse from 1 (so the numbers of the pictures are
not always consecutive), and EXT is png, jpg or pdf.  A picture of PostScript
is copied as {c:picN.eps} and made into {c:picN.pdf}, and the file uses the
second.  With {c:latex_pictures=keep} the name is the full name of the picture
file instead, except for a picture of PostScript.  The file is written to a
temporary name and renamed when it is whole.
